<h2>Three questions that usually decide the choice</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Is the debt disputed?</strong> Admitted or barely-disputed debts reward the pressure routes — summary suit, IBC notice, Samadhaan. Genuinely disputed accounts need a forum that can try facts: a commercial suit or arbitration.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>What leverage exists?</strong> A bounced cheque adds a criminal timeline; MSME status adds compound interest and the pre-deposit shield; an arbitration clause may subtract the court option for the merits while leaving Section 9 interim relief available.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Where are the assets?</strong> A decree or award is only as good as its execution. If the debtor is asset-light, the collective and reputational mechanisms (IBC, interim compensation, interest accumulation) may outperform a paper judgment.</div>
</div>

<h2>Sequencing and parallel tracks</h2>
<p>The routes are not mutually exclusive, and skilled sequencing extracts value from their differences. A common Delhi pattern: a comprehensive demand notice; a Section 138 complaint on the bounced cheque to establish a criminal timeline; and the civil or arbitral claim for the full account filed alongside, with the criminal settlement pressure funding the civil resolution. MSME suppliers add the Samadhaan reference, whose statutory interest at three times the bank rate compounding monthly usually dwarfs contractual interest and reshapes settlement mathematics. What sequencing cannot do is double-recover: payments received in any track must be credited across all, and settlements should record which proceedings stand withdrawn or compounded.</p>

<h2>Limitation: the clock that runs through everything</h2>
<p>Whatever the route, the underlying money claim is generally governed by a three-year limitation period, extendable by written acknowledgment or part-payment. Two traps recur. First, negotiating parties let the period lapse in good faith — a suit or reference filed on time with talks continuing costs little and preserves everything. Second, businesses assume the cheque prosecution keeps the civil claim alive; it does not. Each track has its own clock, and each must be watched.</p>

<h2>The first-month checklist</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>Reconstruct the account: purchase orders, invoices, delivery proofs, ledger confirmations, and every acknowledgment bearing a date and signature.</p>
<p>Audit the leverage: cheques in hand, arbitration clause, Udyam status year-wise, debtor's corporate form and asset position.</p>
<p>Send a notice engineered for the chosen route — the Section 138, IBC and MSMED tracks each have notice requirements that a generic lawyer's letter can fumble.</p>
<p>Diarise limitation for the civil claim independently of every other proceeding.</p>
</div>
